---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import img from '../../assets/images/big-problem.jpg';
---

<Layout title="The Big Problem" description="Why life skills in village children is the root problem NavSahyog Foundation exists to solve.">
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Our Programme"
    title="The Big Problem"
    subtitle="Rural India does not lack potential. It lacks the life skills that turn potential into livelihood."
  />

  <div class="container-content py-16 grid lg:grid-cols-5 gap-12">
    <article class="lg:col-span-3 prose-content">
      <p>
        Salaried jobs in Indian villages are scarce and rarely a path to superior livelihood. For
        rural youth to lift themselves — and their families — out of subsistence, the realistic
        path is <strong>entrepreneurship</strong>: starting small enterprises that solve real, unmet
        needs in their own communities.
      </p>
      <p>
        And there are many such needs. Clean drinking water. Affordable nutritious food. Local
        childcare. Preventive health. Skill services. These are billion-rupee opportunities sitting
        in plain sight, waiting for people with the right mindset to take them on.
      </p>
      <p>
        The problem is not opportunity. The problem is that when you ask a 20-year-old in a village
        today to start something, most cannot. They lack well-developed <strong>self-esteem,
        confidence, the ability to work in teams, a values framework</strong>, and the habits of
        disciplined daily effort. Not because they are less capable — but because no one ever helped
        them build these skills at the age they needed to.
      </p>

      <h2>Why we work with 5–14 year olds</h2>
      <p>
        Life skills are most reliably built during a child's most impressionable years — roughly
        ages 5 to 14. Wait until 18 or 20, and the window has largely closed. Begin at 5, engage
        <strong>every day for a decade</strong>, and by the time those children are young adults,
        they are ready to take on opportunities that their parents could not.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is especially true for girls in rural India, who face the additional challenges of
        being pushed out of school and into early marriages. A girl who has grown up with eight or
        nine years of daily life-skills engagement stands in a very different place.
      </p>

      <h2>What we do about it</h2>
      <p>
        We run a standard, structured <a href="/program/intervention/">daily programme</a> in over
        300 villages — Monday to Saturday, an hour most days and two hours on Saturdays. Sports.
        Storytelling. Experiential learning. Creative activities. Delivered by a team that is 97%
        women, 97% of whom live in the villages they serve.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not a short-term intervention. It is a decade-long bet on every single child who
        joins the programme.
      </p>
    </article>
    <aside class="lg:col-span-2">
      <Image src={img} alt="Village girl" class="rounded-xl w-full h-auto shadow-md" />
      <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-6 bg-surface-soft">
        <p class="text-brand font-semibold uppercase text-sm mb-2">In one sentence</p>
        <p class="text-lg text-ink/90">
          Rural India's opportunity gap is really a life-skills gap. We close it, at the age it can
          still be closed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>
</Layout>
